<Base
  title="The Bunker — Alissa Nicole"
  description="Case study: the Bunker, a personal agentic engineering platform, routing, sandboxing, memory, and measurement for AI agents that do real work."
  current="home"
>
  <p class="backlink"><a href="/">← All projects</a></p>
  <h1>The Bunker</h1>
  <p class="tagline">A personal agentic engineering platform, the infrastructure that lets AI agents plan, build, test, and review real software safely.</p>

  <nav class="toc" aria-label="Contents">
    <a href="#the-problem">The problem</a>
    <a href="#what-it-does">What it does</a>
    <a href="#routing">Routing</a>
    <a href="#safety">Safety</a>
    <a href="#memory">Memory</a>
    <a href="#measurement">Measurement</a>
    <a href="#workflow">How work flows</a>
    <a href="#limits">Honest limits</a>
  </nav>

  <h2 id="the-problem">The problem</h2>
  <p>
    One prompt is cheap; trusting the hundredth is expensive. AI coding agents are
    genuinely capable now, but using them seriously is an infrastructure problem, not
    a prompting problem. Which model gets which task? What is the agent allowed to
    touch? What did that session cost? Where does its knowledge come from, and how do
    you know it actually read what you gave it? Without answers, you get confident
    garbage at scale. The model is the easy part; everything that makes its output
    trustworthy has to be built.
  </p>

  <h2 id="what-it-does">What it does</h2>
  <p>
    The Bunker is my personal engineering environment, designed so AI agents can do
    real work, specs, tickets, TDD loops, reviews, under explicit guardrails. Every
    task is routed to the right class of model. Every tool call sits behind an approval
    gate and a sandbox. Every metered session is logged with its token cost. And all knowledge
    lives in shared stores that humans and agents read and write through the same
    conventions. The design rule is the same one as in <a href="/projects/lci-graph">LCI·GRAPH</a>:
    <strong>AI drafts, rules decide, humans approve.</strong>
  </p>

  <figure>
    <img src="/snapshots/bunker/bunker-stack.svg" alt="The Bunker, top to bottom: a human sets intent and owns the merge; a frontier-class planner model turns specs into tickets and reviews output; a cheaper executor model runs headless TDD loops in git worktrees; a guardrails band, quota guard, Docker sandbox, approval gate, applies to every call; and a memory band, knowledge layer over MCP and a usage ledger, makes everything compound and everything counted." loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption>
      The whole machine, top to bottom. Blue is where models run, grey is deterministic
      code, orange is me. Each layer below gets its close-up.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2 id="routing">Routing, the right model for the job</h2>
  <p>
    Not every task deserves a frontier model, and not every task survives a cheap one.
    The Bunker splits work by class: a strong <strong>planner</strong> writes specs,
    breaks them into tickets, and reviews what got built; a cheaper <strong>executor</strong>
    runs the headless TDD loops, roughly 10% of a ticket's token burn goes to the
    planner, the rest to the executor. A shared quota guard gates every session: a live
    read of the provider's usage API on the CLI, a short-cached one on the server,
    blocking above configurable thresholds, fail-closed, because an unreachable usage
    API is not a reason to spend blind.
  </p>
  <p>
    Routing decisions are audited against actuals, not vibes. Every session appends to
    a usage ledger, model, tokens, estimated cost, which ticket it served:
  </p>
  <pre><code>{ledgerSnippet}</code></pre>
  <p>
    If execution tokens start showing up on the planner's model, the summary makes the
    drift visible within a week. Compliance is auditable because the ledger exists.
  </p>

  <h2 id="safety">Safety is a floor, not a feature</h2>
  <p>
    Agents are advice-only by default, they propose, I dispose. Tool execution is
    granted per request, never per session, and when it's granted, shell commands run
    inside a Docker sandbox with the working directory mounted in. Secrets live in one
    central place and are never copied into project directories; a project-local config
    that overrides a fresh token is a bug I've already fixed once. The gate is the
    product: autonomy is something the system earns per request, not something it gets
    by being launched.
  </p>

  <h2 id="memory">Memory that compounds</h2>
  <p>
    Agents are only as good as the context they're handed, so the Bunker treats
    knowledge as infrastructure. Notes are cheap to capture and designed for
    rediscovery: a tag taxonomy with tolerant routing into a central project registry,
    unique entry IDs, freshness conventions. Decisions live in specs with decision
    logs, so the <em>why</em> survives the session that made it. Agents reach all of it
    through MCP servers, the same stores I use, the same write paths. And every
    project carries a handoff file, so the next session starts where this one ended
    instead of re-reading the world.
  </p>

  <h2 id="measurement">Measured, not asserted</h2>
  <p>
    Two disciplines keep the system honest. First, an eval harness for the thing agents
    actually fail at, reading provided context, designed and now being built: graded
    0–4 checks, golden needles planted in documents, negative-claim audits, deterministic
    checks before any model-graded ones. Second, a review loop with teeth, already in
    practice: executor work is not "done" when tests go green, the planner re-reads the
    output against the ticket's intent, because tests prove the cases pass, not that the
    design is clean. Green is necessary, not sufficient.
  </p>

  <h2 id="workflow">How work flows</h2>
  <p>
    A feature starts as a short decisions-table spec. The spec breaks into vertical
    tracer-bullet tickets, each one a thin slice through every layer, demonstrable
    end-to-end. Tickets go to the headless executor, a topology validated on real
    ticket work, which loops red-green-refactor; verification and a planner review
    pass follow; commits stay scoped to the ticket. Parallel execution across git
    worktrees is spec'd as the next step. Every ticket ends with a handoff note for
    the next session, because I clear context between sessions and the system has to
    remember what I don't. Feedback loops stay short, integration risk surfaces
    immediately, and every increment is shippable-ish.
  </p>

  <h2 id="limits">Honest limits</h2>
  <p>
    This is a single-user system at personal scale, not a 200-engineer monorepo, and
    I won't pretend the numbers are anything else. The ledger has a known blind spot,
    recorded in the spec: advice-only sessions leave no token trace, so their cost is
    estimated from payload size instead of measured. What transfers is the topology, the
    gates, and the measurement discipline. What I'd test first at company scale:
    whether the quota guard's fail-closed default survives a team that hits limits
    daily, and whether per-request approval stays a gate or becomes a rubber stamp
    under deadline pressure. Those are the right questions; I'd bring the same falsifiable-hypothesis
    discipline I used for <a href="/projects/lci-graph">the graph layer</a> to answer them.
  </p>
</Base>
